WEBVTT

1
00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:56.040
So, hello.

2
00:29:56.040 --> 00:30:00.320
I'm an independent artist,
writer, and coder based in the UK.

3
00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:04.120
“FreeThem” is a narrative game web app

4
00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:07.800
that I'm creating using HTML, CSS,
and JavaScript.

5
00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:12.360
It offers or implies a feminist critique
of menstrual tracking apps

6
00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:17.320
and how they shape diverse users’
understandings of their bodies.

7
00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:22.560
This creative work is an output from
an international research project, “The

8
00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:26.840
Intimate Technology Shaping
Millions of Lives,” led by Sarah Riley,

9
00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:31.120
professor of critical health psychology
at Massey University in New Zealand.

10
00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:36.600
And the members of the research team
are on the screen.

11
00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:42.400
Millions of people worldwide use digital
apps to monitor their menstrual cycles.

12
00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:46.720
These apps can be experienced
as empowering, but they're not neutral.

13
00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:51.840
They play a role in how users understand
their bodies and by extension,

14
00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:55.040
their selves, their relationships,
and their place in the world.

15
00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:59.840
Importantly, menstrual tracking apps
are designed around a prototypical user:

16
00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:04.440
a white, affluent, cisgender,
heterosexual, able-bodied young woman,

17
00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:10.280
which reinforces narrow, potentially
harmful ideas about normal or ideal

18
00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:11.760
bodies.

19
00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:16.680
The research project asks,
“What are the experience of experiences

20
00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:21.560
of the many non-prototypical users
whose bodies, identities, or life stages

21
00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:27.080
are marginalized by these apps?” So,
because “menstrual tracking

22
00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:31.840
app” is quite a mouthful,
I'll use MTA for short from now on.

23
00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:34.840
So the research team conducted

24
00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:38.920
participatory research
with MTA users in New Zealand.

25
00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:43.360
They focused on three life stage cohorts:
16-to-18-year-olds,

26
00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:46.520
postnatal, and perimenopausal groups.

27
00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:49.640
They were particularly interested
in talking to people who are less

28
00:31:49.640 --> 00:31:52.920
likely to be represented in research,
including indigenous

29
00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:58.120
Māori and takatāpui people—
Excuse my pronunciation.

30
00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:02.040
People managing chronic health
conditions or disability.

31
00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:05.840
People identifying as fat or experiencing
weight stigma, and

32
00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:09.120
or who are gender or sexually diverse.

33
00:32:09.640 --> 00:32:14.680
The findings give unprecedented insight
into how MTAs shape diverse users

34
00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:18.560
experiences of their bodies,
and they are generating ideas

35
00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:22.640
on how these apps might be designed
to be affirmative and inclusive.

36
00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:25.840
Another important output of the research

37
00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:28.880
is the story game web app
that I'm creating.

38
00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:32.920
It's for mobile first,
but all screen sizes eventually.

39
00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:36.560
And here on-screen is my creative brief.

40
00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:41.880
So “FreeThem” is a creative expression
of the research findings.

41
00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:46.480
Research with the postnatal
and perimenopausal groups

42
00:32:46.480 --> 00:32:49.280
went ahead as planned,
but the teenage cohort

43
00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:52.360
has taken much longer
and the data isn't ready yet.

44
00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:56.960
So initially, I was asked to create a
story driven app focusing on postnatal and

45
00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:00.080
menopausal users of MTAs.

46
00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:04.960
However, the team are keen to include
16-to-18-year-olds, so subject to funding,

47
00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:08.400
I'll be incorporating those stories
at a later date.

48
00:33:09.160 --> 00:33:12.200
So some of the main themes
from the research in those

49
00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:16.400
in the postnatal and perimenopausal groups
are the apps are paradoxical.

50
00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:19.440
There's a tension between care
versus control.

51
00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:23.960
There are— the apps are experienced
as empowering, but at the same time,

52
00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:27.120
non-prototypical users
experience exclusions and misfitting.

53
00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:31.400
And the apps can exacerbate
existing inequities that reinforce

54
00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:35.120
stigmatizing norms
and the pressure to regulate oneself.

55
00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:38.480
The apps also reinforce
the reproductive centered

56
00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:43.320
biomedical paradigm
by prioritizing fertility and pregnancy.

57
00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:47.600
There's an implicit sense
of perimenopausal “crones” versus

58
00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:49.760
celebrated mothers.

59
00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:52.920
And there's a big issue around trust
and de-trust of both apps

60
00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:54.640
and medical authority.

61
00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:57.760
For example, a frequent complaint through

62
00:33:57.800 --> 00:34:00.960
the research is of doctors not listening,

63
00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:04.680
so users need the apps as an advocacy tool

64
00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:09.120
to collect data to track symptoms,
as well as to do things like get pregnant.

65
00:34:09.520 --> 00:34:13.280
But they're also aware that they can't
necessarily trust these commercial apps

66
00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:17.480
with their private data,
which can be exploited in so many ways.

67
00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:23.120
Also, health is hard
work, and MTAs tend to add to this burden.

68
00:34:24.760 --> 00:34:27.800
So I'm of a generation
that didn't have access to MTAs.

69
00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:32.680
I've never used one,
so I researched a variety of apps, MTAs,

70
00:34:32.680 --> 00:34:36.280
and tried them by entering fake data, etc.

71
00:34:36.320 --> 00:34:40.360
and what struck me is the vast majority
look remarkably similar.

72
00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:42.280
Pinks and

73
00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:44.840
pastel colors really dominate.

74
00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:47.680
They also have a predilection
for representing

75
00:34:47.680 --> 00:34:50.680
app users in this flat cartoon style.

76
00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:54.400
There seems to be an attempt
to represent diverse users

77
00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:57.440
and body types,
but the cartoon style style

78
00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:01.760
is homogenizing and erases
bodily human imperfections.

79
00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:05.280
So I wanted to emulate
this kind of aesthetics.

80
00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:10.320
I wanted to design a story game app
that initially and superficially resembles

81
00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:13.520
a menstrual tracking app
to lure the player in.

82
00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:19.320
So the characters for this on-screen
now is my app.

83
00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:23.120
The characters are fictional, but they're
based on the research participants.

84
00:35:23.120 --> 00:35:25.760
Each character is a few participants,

85
00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:28.760
kind of blended together
that kind of composites.

86
00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:32.760
As they currently stand,
there are three postnatal characters:

87
00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:37.360
Lucy, Danny, and Finley,
who is non-binary; and three

88
00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:42.640
perimenopausal characters:
Arona—who is Maori—Zoe, and Claire.

89
00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:45.400
Each character has an

90
00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:49.680
associated casual game,
whose procedural rhetoric expresses

91
00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:53.120
some of the frustrations
of using menstrual tracking apps.

92
00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:58.520
Some games are based on overt
or explicit features of typical MTAs:

93
00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:01.680
period logging,
identifying the symptoms of PMS

94
00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:05.760
or perimenopause,
predicting facility type cycles.

95
00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:09.600
Other games speak to more hidden
or covert characteristics.

96
00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:13.960
As I said, using MTAs is hard work,
adding to the pressures of daily life

97
00:36:14.080 --> 00:36:18.000
at companies that sell or abuse users’
private data.

98
00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:21.520
The app encourages you to play the games.

99
00:36:21.520 --> 00:36:25.400
It has a particular voice, which
I think of as another distinct character,

100
00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:30.560
and privately
I characterize that as the “MTA Wizard,”

101
00:36:30.680 --> 00:36:35.360
and I'm still working on that aspect
because it actually is a work in progress.

102
00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:39.960
The app tries to distract you
from the characters’ stories.

103
00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:44.080
It wants you to play the games
much in the same way that commercial apps

104
00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:47.360
want to capture user's attention,
but the women

105
00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:51.240
and the non-binary person
want to communicate their experiences.

106
00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:53.000
They want you to listen to them.

107
00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:55.880
So as you play and

108
00:36:55.880 --> 00:36:59.560
learn more about the fictional characters,
the aesthetic changes.

109
00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:03.080
The representation of the individuals
becomes more rounded,

110
00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:04.840
not necessarily more realistic,

111
00:37:04.840 --> 00:37:08.600
but they become more visually rounded
as the characters become more rounded.

112
00:37:08.600 --> 00:37:09.680
In narrative terms,

113
00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:13.480
as you, the player, learn more about them
and their experiences.

114
00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:17.320
The point is that as you listen to what
the women and non-binary person

115
00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:21.760
have to say, they can begin to escape
or emerge from the prism

116
00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:24.800
of flat caricature.

117
00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:28.040
So I want to play some video

118
00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:31.800
recordings of the app
in this mobile device format.

119
00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:35.240
So the first panel that you can see is

120
00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:39.600
takes you through the player
experience of the Lucy game story.

121
00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:42.880
Lucy is a postnatal character.

122
00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:01.720
It is possible

123
00:38:01.720 --> 00:38:05.400
to win the game,
but not in that particular iteration.

124
00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:10.600
So here's Lucy's story

125
00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:14.480
and will be scrolling quickly
through it in a moment.

126
00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:17.680
But here's the app

127
00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:20.680
trying to get you to go back to the game.

128
00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:25.320
So as you read through Lucy's story,
you've come to the end

129
00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:29.280
where you you can free her,
you can set Lucy free,

130
00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:33.640
and then she encourages you to go
and help the others in the app.

131
00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:37.640
So I'll just play you some of the other

132
00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:41.400
characters’ games,
just briefly, just to give you an idea.

133
00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:46.040
So Arona is— Ooh, sorry.

134
00:38:51.520 --> 00:38:54.520
Arona is a perimenopausal,

135
00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:58.320
and Zoe is also perimenopausal.

136
00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:11.040
So some of the games are winnable

137
00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:14.040
and some aren't.

138
00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:35.440
So here we have Finley, who's postnatal,

139
00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:39.280
Claire is perimenopausal,
and Danny is postnatal.

140
00:39:54.440 --> 00:39:57.400
So there will be a global game
scoring system

141
00:39:57.400 --> 00:40:01.400
that will track how many games
you've kind of won or lose— are lost.

142
00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:06.200
I haven't yet implemented that,
but basically that's not the real point.

143
00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:07.360
That's a distraction.

144
00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:10.360
The important thing is listening
to the people, listening

145
00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:13.400
to the characters.

146
00:40:15.760 --> 00:40:17.800
So when you've freed

147
00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:22.880
all the characters,
you get to the ending where the characters

148
00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:28.480
collectively express what they want 
and how they envisage—

149
00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:32.240
what possibilities they envisage
for these technologies in the future

150
00:40:32.240 --> 00:40:35.240
and the wider health and social setting.

151
00:40:35.800 --> 00:40:38.800
So that's where the project is at at
the moment.

152
00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:41.760
I will be

153
00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:46.720
putting online a private beta testing
version early next month.

154
00:40:46.760 --> 00:40:51.600
So if you'd like to— That's basically
for the research team and participants.

155
00:40:51.600 --> 00:40:55.920
But if you'd like to take part
and give us some feedback, I'd be very—

156
00:40:56.920 --> 00:40:58.160
I'd like that a lot.

157
00:40:58.160 --> 00:41:01.160
So I'll put my

158
00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:05.400
email in the chat.

159
00:41:15.880 --> 00:41:18.360
If you'd like to.

160
00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:19.720
And I'll send you the link.

161
00:41:19.720 --> 00:41:22.400
And then you'll be able
to test the app as well.

162
00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:24.640
Thank you.
